# Line-ending policy.
#
# `core.autocrlf` is enabled at the system level on at least one development
# machine (Git for Windows sets it in the system gitconfig). Without an explicit
# policy here, checked-out shell scripts and hook files receive CRLF and fail in
# Git Bash with `$'\r': command not found`.
#
# The working tree is already LF-only, so this file records existing behavior
# rather than changing it.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-native launchers are the one place CRLF is required.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Adapter fixtures encode exact command-file bytes. The Windows cleanup tests
# assert on process-tree behaviour driven by these files, so normalizing their
# line endings would change what is actually being exercised. The committed
# fixture uses LF and passes; leave it byte-identical on every checkout.
packages/adapters/test-fixtures/** -text

# Never normalize binary content.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.zip binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
